From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Salah Ahmed <haddad2inc@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ath79: ar8216: skip PHYs on another switch's internal MDIO bus


CONFIG_AR8216_PHY is part of the ath79 target default and its phy_driver
matches on 0x004d0000/0xffff0000, which covers the PHY ID of the
QCA83xx port PHYs (0x004dd036 on a QCA8337 rev. 2). It therefore also
claims the PHYs that qca8k exposes on a switch's internal MDIO bus once
a board is converted to DSA.

That is fatal on ath79. 730-ar8216-make-reg-access-atomic.patch wraps
every register access in local_irq_save()/local_irq_restore() and warns
that it "breaks the driver on any mdio master with interrupts used".
qca8k's MDIO master is exactly such a master: it takes a mutex, may
allocate and transmit skbs (qca8k_phy_eth_command) and busy-waits up to
QCA8K_BUSY_WAIT_TIMEOUT (2000 ms) per transaction, while
ar8xxx_read_id() retries the whole sequence AR8X16_PROBE_RETRIES times.
On a single-core SoC that runs with interrupts disabled, which locks
the core up and resets the board in a loop before preinit.

Skip PHYs whose MDIO bus is parented by an mdio_device that is not
bound to this driver: that is the internal bus of a switch owned by
another driver. The SoC MDIO masters (ag71xx, mdio-gpio, mdio-bitbang)
are parented by platform devices, and our own ar8xxx-mdio bus is
exempted by comparing the parent's driver, so swconfig boards -
including the built-in switches described by the "mdio-bus" child node
in qca956x.dtsi, qca953x.dtsi, ar7240.dtsi and ar9330.dtsi - are
unaffected.

mdio_bus_type is private to phylib and is not exported, so the parent
is recognised by its bus name instead of by its bus_type; that keeps
the patch buildable whether ar8216 is built in or as a module.

The patch lives in the ath79 tree because the lock-up needs the
interrupt-disabling accessors added by
730-ar8216-make-reg-access-atomic.patch, and CONFIG_AR8216_PHY is set
by target/linux/ath79/config-6.18 (the mikrotik subtarget turns it off
in its config-default, which is why those boards never see this).

Tested on a COMFAST CF-WA350 (QCA9563 single core, external QCA8337
rev. 2 on SGMII, Linux 6.18.52) converted to qca8k/DSA with the port
PHYs on the switch's internal MDIO bus. With this patch the board
boots: the PHYs are enumerated at internal addresses 0 and 1 as
"Qualcomm Atheros 8337 internal PHY", "DSA: tree 0 setup" completes and
both user ports link at 1000 Mb/s Full with the hardware MIB counters
incrementing under traffic. The same board also boots with the legacy
mapping, where qca8k enumerates the port PHYs at addresses 1 and 2:
ar8xxx_phy_probe() rejects both before touching a register, so that
layout needs no guard at all. Adding an explicit "mdio" child moves the
same PHYs to addresses 0 and 1, address 0 passes the filter, and only
then does the driver reach ar8xxx_read() - which is exactly the access
this patch prevents.

This is the interference that 7d1c5a68e5 ("ath79: convert
qca9558_tplink_archer-c.dtsi to swconfig") names explicitly and that
made it revert the Archer C series; #12864 describes the same two
drivers as mutually exclusive on this target. With the guard in place a
board can keep CONFIG_AR8216_PHY built in and still put its QCA83xx port
PHYs on the switch's internal bus.

Signed-off-by: Salah Ahmed <haddad2inc@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/phy/ar8216.c | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/phy/ar8216.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/ar8216.c
@@ -37,6 +37,9 @@
 
 #include "ar8216.h"
 
+/* defined at the bottom of this file, used to recognise our own MDIO bus */
+static struct mdio_driver ar8xxx_mdio_driver;
+
 extern const struct ar8xxx_chip ar8327_chip;
 extern const struct ar8xxx_chip ar8337_chip;
 
@@ -2611,6 +2614,34 @@ ar8xxx_phy_probe(struct phy_device *phyd
 	if (phydev->mdio.addr != 0 && phydev->mdio.addr != 3 && phydev->mdio.addr != 4)
 		return -ENODEV;
 
+	/*
+	 * Never claim a PHY that sits on the internal MDIO bus of a switch
+	 * driven by another driver, such as the bus qca8k registers for the
+	 * QCA83xx port PHYs once a board is converted to DSA.
+	 *
+	 * The register accessors below disable interrupts around every
+	 * transaction (730-ar8216-make-reg-access-atomic.patch), which is only
+	 * safe on the polling-mode MDIO masters used on ath79. The MDIO master
+	 * of such a switch instead takes a mutex, may allocate and transmit
+	 * skbs and busy-waits up to 2000 ms per access, so probing through it
+	 * with interrupts off locks up a single-core SoC and resets the
+	 * board in a loop before preinit.
+	 *
+	 * Such a bus is parented by the switch's mdio_device, while the SoC
+	 * MDIO masters (ag71xx, mdio-gpio, mdio-bitbang) are parented by
+	 * platform devices. Our own ar8xxx-mdio bus is exempted by comparing
+	 * the parent's driver.
+	 *
+	 * mdio_bus_type is private to phylib and is not exported, so the bus
+	 * is recognised by its name instead of by its bus_type.
+	 */
+	if (phydev->mdio.bus->parent &&
+	    phydev->mdio.bus->parent->bus &&
+	    !strcmp(phydev->mdio.bus->parent->bus->name, "mdio_bus") &&
+	    phydev->mdio.bus->parent->driver !=
+			&ar8xxx_mdio_driver.mdiodrv.driver)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
 	if (!ar8xxx_is_possible(phydev->mdio.bus))
 		return -ENODEV;
 
